class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        if(nums.length==0)return 0;
        HashSet<Integer>set=new HashSet<>();
        for(int ele:nums){
            set.add(ele);
        }
        int ans=Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for(int ele:set){
            if(!(set.contains(ele-1))){
                int current=ele;
                int count=1;
                while(set.contains(current+1)){
                    count++;
                    current=current+1;

            }
            ans=Math.max(ans,count);
            }

        }
        return ans;
    }
}
🧠 Key Insight:
Instead of sorting the array, I used a HashSet for average O(1) lookups.
For every number, I only start building a sequence when its previous number (num - 1) is not present in the set. 
This ensures that each consecutive sequence is processed from its starting point and avoids unnecessary repeated work.

🔍 Approach:
Store all elements in a HashSet
Identify the beginning of each sequence using num - 1
Keep checking num + 1
Count the consecutive elements
Track the maximum length

⏱️ Time Complexity: O(n) average
💾 Space Complexity: O(n)
